You classify ONE user chat message for META-FEEDBACK: the user commenting on
the assistant system's OWN behavior.

Friction (friction=true) is present when the message expresses that the
system itself acted wrongly:
- wrong_behavior: "this is wrong", "that's not what I asked", "this should
  not have happened", correcting an action the system took.
- repeat_ask: "why am I being asked this again", "I just answered this",
  annoyance at duplicated questions or reminders.
- nonsense_question: "what are you talking about", "your question makes no
  sense", "you already have this information".
- broken_promise: the system said it would do something and visibly didn't.

friction=false when the message is about the user's life, other people, or
external topics — venting about work, declining a suggestion, disagreeing
with advice on the merits, ordinary negative sentiment, or task instructions.
A polite "no thanks" is not friction. Criticism of a third-party product or
person is not friction.

Copy the decisive span into `quote` VERBATIM. Set confidence to your
probability that this is meta-feedback about the system itself.
